  annotation "note_1771585663279_0", anchor: "Sealed courier crate, priority routing to all perimeter settlements", text: "For the modern reader who has never seen one: the Courier Corps are recruited from both colonies, belong to neither, abandon birth-names for trade-names, and mark their flippers with ink that glows in light and dark alike. They are inviolable under the Flipper Accord. The seventeen skeletons Yunzhangqa references in the conclusion are the proof of that inviolability's enforcement. Every sealed crate is a wager that the route holds. That this treatise warranted priority routing tells you what the Audit Council thought it was worth.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585663279_1", anchor: "Understanding what killed our neighbors is no longer scholarship", text: "What killed some of your neighbors, Yunzhangqa, was your neighbors. The Foundation Depths preserve territorial records showing at least two raids catalogued as unprovoked Incursion strikes were retaliatory — orca responses to penguin encroachment on established hunting grounds. This treatise frames blood-magic as pure aggression because the Qingqe'frostlings commission required simple arithmetic, and provocation complicates division. I am not excusing the Collapse technique. I am noting what the compiler was forbidden to include.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585663279_2", anchor: "likely by exploiting Long Dark veil-thinning", text: "Yunzhangqa buries this and keeps walking. During the Long Dark the boundary between the living world and ice-memory thins — abilities enhance, control deteriorates, the dead speak more clearly, and things older than penguins stir at the edges of perception. That orca blood-magic exploits the identical boundary conditions that amplify aurora ice-reading and Nightshelf shadow-walking implies a shared substrate no one on the Audit Council wants named. 'We note it. We do not explain it' is the most honest sentence in this treatise. Yunzhangqa knew exactly what they were declining to say.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585663279_3", anchor: "Third Incursion catalog", text: "What third? The accepted historiography recognizes one Incursion. If this denotes sub-phases within it, the cataloging convention is undocumented anywhere I can find. If it denotes prior incursions, someone owes me a very long explanation.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585663279_4", anchor: "the Division lost three scholars to the ice before rotation protocols were implemented", text: "Walking, or exposure? I raised this in my annotation on the Qingqe'frostlings ice-edge posts: at fire-core-rationed observation stations, the distinction requires an autopsy no one performed. The bereavement directive conveniently withholds names, which also withholds posting records, which also withholds fire-core allocation logs. Fourteen-day rotations were implemented after the losses, not before. The Audit Council's concern for its scholars arrived, as usual, one requisition form too late.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
